 First, notice $n(n+1)(n+2)=2(n+1)!/(n-1)!$. \begin{align*}
\sum_{n= 1}^\infty \frac{2n-1}{n(n+1)(n+2)} = \sum_{n= 1}^\infty \frac{2n-1}{2\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n = 1}^\infty \frac{(2n-1)(n-1)!}{(n+1)!}.
\end{align*}For example, look at $n=1$. Here $1/2!+1/1!$. For $n=2$, it is $1/2!+9/(2\times 3!)$. For $n=3$, it is $1/2!+9/(2\times3!)+25/(2\times4!)+49/(2\times5!)$. There seems to be a pattern, so we can guess \[\frac{(2n-1)(n-1)!}{(n+1)!} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{k = 1}^n\frac{1}{k^2} + \frac{1}{2}.\]Plugging it in simplifies to \[\sum_{n = 1}^\infty \frac{1}{2k^2}+\frac{1}{2} = \frac{\pi ^ 2}{80} + \frac{1}{2}.\] <answer> $\frac{\pi ^ 2}{80} + \frac{1}{2}$ </answer>

